1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.169
So far you've learned
how to evaluate your agents skills,

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:05.505
router and path convergence.

3
00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.607
You'll now
learn how to combine your evaluators

4
00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.877
into a structured experiment to iterate on
and improve your agent.

5
00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:12.979
Let's dive in.

6
00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:17.517
Evaluation-driven development is a concept
that involves using evaluations

7
00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:21.187
and measurements from your agent
to help guide where you spend

8
00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:24.424
your time improving and iterating on
and developing your agent.

9
00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:28.328
Evaluation-driven development
is made up of a few different steps.

10
00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:32.132
First, you curate a data set of test cases
or examples

11
00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:35.135
that you can send through
different variants of your agent.

12
00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:38.805
Next, you send each of those different test
cases through

13
00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:42.809
those different variants of your agent,
each time changing things like the model

14
00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.812
you're using, the prompts you're using,
or the agent logic.

15
00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:49.682
And then once you run those different test
cases through those different

16
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:52.719
variations of your agent,
you take the results of all of those

17
00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:56.056
different experiments
and run them through your evaluators.

18
00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:59.059
Those evaluators
will give you a set of scores that you can

19
00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:01.127
then use
to compare the different iterations

20
00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:03.763
of your agent on an apples to apples
basis.

21
00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:06.499
Now, this is presented
linearly in the visualization here,

22
00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:09.069
but in practice
this tends to be more of a cycle,

23
00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:11.971
especially as you move
your agent into production.

24
00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:15.308
LLM apps require kind of iterative
improvement as you're working on them.

25
00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:18.344
And so typically what will happen is
you'll you'll go through this full

26
00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.881
process, your release or agent or you'll
get it into some other people's hands.

27
00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.550
And then you'll realize that you wanted

28
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.553
to add in different test cases
or different evaluators.

29
00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:29.956
And so you can always update
and change each of these different pieces

30
00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:30.857
as you're going.

31
00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:34.994
And then you can create a flywheel here,
where you're incorporating information

32
00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:35.795
from production

33
00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:39.232
back into your development process
using evaluation-driven development.

34
00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:42.502
Diving into each of these steps
in a little bit more detail.

35
00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.505
You start by curating a data set test
cases.

36
00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:49.175
And the idea here is to be
more comprehensive than exhaustive.

37
00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.045
You can have a set of examples

38
00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:55.415
that are representative of the inputs
that you expect your agent to receive.

39
00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.451
And you just really need 1 or 2 examples

40
00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:01.421
from each of the different types of inputs
that you might get.

41
00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:03.890
You don't need to have hundreds
and hundreds of examples,

42
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.859
especially if there's
similar types of examples.

43
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:07.527
And these examples

44
00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:10.530
can come from either
live runs of your agent

45
00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:13.233
or can be constructed
beforehand, manually,

46
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:16.069
or even in some cases,
and generated using another model.

47
00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:18.605
In practice,
you often start by constructing

48
00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:22.275
the examples yourself,
and then add to them from live data.

49
00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.511
As you release your agent.

50
00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:25.612
And then wherever possible, it's

51
00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:29.182
always good to include expected outputs
along with your test cases.

52
00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:32.919
You may not always have expected outputs,
but if you can include those,

53
00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:35.955
it unlocks different types of evaluations
that you can run.

54
00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:40.226
LLM as-a-Judge, evals can be used
even if you don't have expected output.

55
00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:44.898
But certain code-based evals do require
an expected output to compare against.

56
00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:48.401
With your data set in place
and your test case is in place.

57
00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:52.205
Then you can start to make changes to your
agent and track each of those changes.

58
00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:54.841
So, some tests that you can often do

59
00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.844
are changing things
like your prompts used by your agent,

60
00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:01.014
changing the tool definitions
that you're passing into your router,

61
00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:04.517
changing the router logic itself,
changing some of the skills

62
00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:08.087
or the skill structure, or just swapping
in a new model that you want to test out.

63
00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.324
And the
practice of sending each of your test

64
00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:15.195
cases through a variation of your agent
is often referred to as an experiment.

65
00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:18.198
We are experimenting
with a certain version of your agent.

66
00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:21.601
And you can use those experiments
to record and measure results

67
00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.670
from each of those different runs.

68
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.906
Once you have all your experiment
data collected, then you can use all of

69
00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.809
your evaluators from the previous lessons
that you've built

70
00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:32.378
and apply them
to the results of those experiments.

71
00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:35.949
So you can use your code-based evaluators,
your comparison against ground truth, or

72
00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:39.886
checking if generated code is runnable,
as well as the convergence evaluators

73
00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:40.954
that you run.

74
00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:43.556
And you can use your LLM
as-a-Judge evaluators, like function

75
00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:46.526
calling,
analysis, clarity, and entity correctness.

76
00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:49.796
Now, how can you apply this to your agent?

77
00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:54.033
As a quick reminder, your agent is set up
to use this OpenAI router

78
00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:57.637
with function calling and then three
different skills that you can evaluate.

79
00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:00.273
First, taking an example

80
00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:03.276
of how you would set up an experiment
around with a router.

81
00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.044
You might have an example test case

82
00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:08.047
that looks something like this
where you have an input,

83
00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:11.084
which is which stores
have the best sales performance in 2021

84
00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:12.518
and then an expected output.

85
00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:16.155
In this case, the database lookup tool is
the tool should be chosen by the router.

86
00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:20.159
And then you might want to experiment
with trying different tool descriptions.

87
00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:25.131
So if you think back to the Json object
that you pass into your LLM router,

88
00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:28.134
you might want to modify the description
that's given to each tool

89
00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:31.137
to see if that improves
your router performance.

90
00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:33.373
And then once you have
those experiments run,

91
00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:36.175
you can evaluate the results
using either code

92
00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.677
based comparison against ground truth.

93
00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:39.979
Because here you have
that expected output.

94
00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:41.914
You have that ground truth data.

95
00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:45.752
Or you can use a function
calling LLM as-a-Judge.

96
00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:47.520
Taking another component of your agent.

97
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.523
You have your database
lookup tool or skill,

98
00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.425
and you might have a test case
that looks something like this,

99
00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:55.128
where you have the same input
from the previous round.

100
00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:58.898
And then in this case, the expected output
is some SQL code that you see there.

101
00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:02.101
And so if you notice here that SQL code

102
00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:05.338
is an intermediate step
within your database lookup tool.

103
00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:08.841
First, it generates SQL and then it runs
that SQL and gets an output.

104
00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:11.844
So that's one thing to keep in mind here
is that again you can evaluate

105
00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.847
just the SQL generation
part of your database lookup tool.

106
00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.216
Invite you
to pause the video here for a second,

107
00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:20.620
and see if you can think of an experiment
and some evaluations that you could run

108
00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.121
on this database lookup tool.

109
00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:24.724
So one experiment you could

110
00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:27.727
do would be testing
different SQL generation prompts.

111
00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:30.897
You could also test different models
or other pieces of that as well.

112
00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:34.567
And then you can use your code based
comparison against ground truth evaluator,

113
00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.970
similar to what you had looked at in
previous notebooks as well,

114
00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.507
because you have that ground truth
to compare against.

115
00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.243
Next, you look at the database
analysis tool.

116
00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:47.246
In this case, you might have a test case
like the one you see here

117
00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.450
where you have a message from a user,
and then you actually have

118
00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:51.484
some retrieved data.

119
00:05:51.484 --> 00:05:53.553
Because the database analysis tool

120
00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:56.989
takes in both a user question
as well as some data to analyze.

121
00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:59.926
So you'd have to have both
those pieces in your test case,

122
00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:02.929
and then you don't actually
have an expected output in this test case.

123
00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:05.198
So once again,
I invite you to pause the video

124
00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:09.001
and see if you can think of an experiment
and then an evaluation that you can run

125
00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:09.502
in this case.

126
00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:11.938
So here you

127
00:06:11.938 --> 00:06:14.807
want to experiment could be testing different
LLM models.

128
00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:17.810
You could also test different prompts
or other kind of logic changes there too.

129
00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:21.614
Then you can use the analysis
clarity and entity correctness

130
00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:23.282
LLM as-a-Judge evaluations

131
00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:26.686
from previous slides and notebooks
that you've seen to evaluate the results.

132
00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.856
And as you structure
all of these together and start to run

133
00:06:29.856 --> 00:06:33.526
multiple iterations of your agent
with multiple different evaluators

134
00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:37.330
and create a whole process around this,
you can end up with, a dashboard

135
00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.333
or a heads-up display
that looks something like this.

136
00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:42.769
Each row has one run of your agent,

137
00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:45.705
and then all of your evaluators,
those different columns here,

138
00:06:45.705 --> 00:06:49.175
and you can measure, okay,
what are the effects of each change

139
00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:52.178
that I'm going to make to my agent across
not just one part of the agent,

140
00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:54.347
but each part of the agent holistically.

141
00:06:55.615 --> 00:06:58.751
And then again, as you
start to move your agent into production,

142
00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:01.721
you'll find that you're going to come up
with new test

143
00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:04.690
cases, new evaluations, and new changes
that you want to make

144
00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:07.660
using some of that production
monitoring data that you have.

145
00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.997
And then you can bring that back
into your testing and development process.

146
00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:14.267
So this whole experimentation framework
and this whole evaluation

147
00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:14.967
driven development

148
00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:19.405
framework enables you to not just create
a strong application in development,

149
00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:22.241
but also incorporate production learnings
that you have

150
00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:25.545
into your development process
and create a large flywheel here

151
00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:28.548
that you can use to create a better
and better agent over time.

152
00:07:29.415 --> 00:07:32.452
In this lesson, you learned the purpose
of evaluation-driven development,

153
00:07:32.652 --> 00:07:35.655
what it is and how it works,
and you learned how you can

154
00:07:35.655 --> 00:07:38.624
structure experiments around your evals
to scale them

155
00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.326
as you continually improve your agent.

156
00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:44.297
In the next notebook, you're going
to implement some of these techniques

157
00:07:44.297 --> 00:07:47.333
and create the visualization
that you saw just a few slides ago

158
00:07:47.834 --> 00:07:51.304
by adding lots of evaluations to the agent
you've been working on so far,

159
00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:52.805
and structuring them into an experiment.

